<%- include("partials/head") %>
<%- include("partials/header") %>
<%- include("partials/nav") %>

<main class="resume-layout">
  <%- include("partials/contactModal") %>

  <article class="project-detail">
    <header class="card project-detail-hero">
      <a href="/projects" class="project-back-link">Back to projects</a>
      <p class="project-tag">Private Alpha · Next.js / TypeScript / PostgreSQL</p>
      <h1>FFKI Alpha</h1>
      <p>
        A controlled private alpha for an AI-powered fantasy football web app.
        I built it as a broad full-stack foundation with league setup, manager
        accounts, draft, rosters, lineups, waivers, trades, scoring, playoffs,
        admin tools, audit/history logs and provider sync foundations.
      </p>

      <div class="project-detail-actions">
        <!-- TODO: Add the real Render URL when the private alpha URL should be public on the portfolio. -->
        <!-- TODO: Add a GitHub link only if the private repository is meant to be shared with reviewers. -->
        <span class="project-status">Private alpha</span>
        <span class="project-status">Controlled demo</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="project-detail-grid">
      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          FFKI Alpha is my strongest current full-stack project. It is not a
          finished public SaaS product and has no public signup. The goal is a
          controlled alpha environment for a fantasy football league with human
          managers, AI manager foundations and one shared demo manager team for
          private testing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Tech Stack</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Next.js, TypeScript and server-side app routes</li>
          <li>PostgreSQL with Prisma data modeling</li>
          <li>Neon PostgreSQL and Render deployment</li>
          <li>Docker for local PostgreSQL development</li>
          <li>GitHub for version control and project work</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Main Features</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Private alpha auth/session foundation and manager accounts</li>
          <li>10-team league setup with human managers and AI manager foundations</li>
          <li>Snake draft foundation with roster ownership</li>
          <li>Lineup slots, waiver claims and trade workflows</li>
          <li>Scoring, matchups, standings and playoff bracket foundation</li>
          <li>Admin/commissioner tools with audit logs and league history</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Architecture Decisions</h2>
        <p>
          The app follows a modular monolith approach. Separate service areas
          handle auth, league setup, managers, teams, draft, roster, lineup,
          waivers, trades, scoring, playoffs, admin tools, audit/history and
          data providers. This keeps the project broad but still understandable
          while the alpha grows.
        </p>
        <ol class="engineering-flow" aria-label="FFKI Alpha engineering flow">
          <li>Auth/session</li>
          <li>League and teams</li>
          <li>Draft and roster</li>
          <li>Waivers/trades</li>
          <li>Scoring/playoffs</li>
          <li>Admin/audit</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Data Provider Approach</h2>
        <p>
          Normal app usage reads from FFKI's internal database. External football
          data is not read live during normal page/API requests. The provider
          sync foundation writes player metadata into internal tables first, and
          the Sleeper player metadata import is currently focused on identity,
          position, team abbreviation, status and bye week.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Admin and Audit</h2>
        <p>
          Admin actions are intentionally traceable. Draft mutations, waiver
          processing, trade review/execution, scoring changes, playoff generation
          and guest-control actions write audit/history records so alpha tests
          can be reviewed instead of disappearing into server output only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Demo Access</h2>
        <p>
          The alpha has a shared demo manager account for one test team. It is
          meant for controlled testing only; demo changes may be reset. The demo
          email is <code>demo@ffki-alpha.local</code>. I do not publish the demo
          password directly on the portfolio page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Current Limitations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Private alpha only; no public signup</li>
          <li>No official NFL/team logos or player images</li>
          <li>No real live NFL scoring/stat import yet</li>
          <li>No real production AI/LLM calls yet</li>
          <li>AI layer is currently proposal/fallback oriented</li>
          <li>External sync writes into the internal DB before normal app usage</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>What I Learned</h2>
        <p>
          FFKI Alpha helped me practice full-stack system thinking: schema
          design, service boundaries, private alpha auth, admin workflows,
          database-backed history, provider sync, deployment constraints and the
          discipline of being honest about what is implemented versus planned.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card project-detail-section">
        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>More complete manager-facing draft and roster flows</li>
          <li>More robust guest/demo controls and reset behavior</li>
          <li>Real scoring/stat import strategy after the alpha foundation is stable</li>
          <li>AI manager behavior through validated proposals before execution</li>
          <li>More screenshots once the private alpha UI is ready to show publicly</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </section>
  </article>
</main>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
